---
// Long-form SEO content section for the homepage. Written for humans first —
// it explains how the tool works and why each option exists — while
// naturally covering the target keyword set across proper heading levels.
---

<section id="about-lorem-ipsum" class="border-b border-hairline bg-canvas py-20 sm:py-28">
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-[800px] px-6">
		<p class="mono-eyebrow mb-3 text-mute">The complete guide</p>
		<h2 class="text-heading-lg text-pretty text-ink">
			Built for real workflows
		</h2>

		<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-6 text-body-md text-body">
			<p>
				This tool gives designers, developers, and writers exact control over
				their placeholder text. Instead of pasting the same fixed block of
				Latin from an old bookmark, you configure a fresh output every
				time — choosing the vocabulary, the amount, and the export format before
				you copy a single character.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				Generate by paragraph, word, or exact character count
			</h3>
			<p>
				Beyond paragraphs, there's a word mode for short labels and buttons,
				a sentence mode for captions, and a dedicated character-count mode
				for the cases those don't cover. Need an exact length for a meta
				description, a tweet, or a fixed-width form field? Set the unit to
				Characters, enter a number like 500, and the output is trimmed to
				that exact length — precisely 500 characters, not "close enough,"
				which matters when you're testing truncation, line wrapping, or
				database column limits.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				More than Latin: themed word banks, including a funny option
			</h3>
			<p>
				Classic Latin is the default for a reason — its letter and word-length
				distribution reads naturally without distracting reviewers from the
				actual design. But Latin doesn't always match the tone of the product
				you're mocking up, so this tool ships five alternate word banks:
				Corporate jargon for business decks, Tech/Startup vocabulary for
				developer tools, Foodie language for menu and recipe layouts,
				Cat-themed filler for playful projects, and — for when a design
				review needs some levity — a Funny mode built from absurd, humorous
				filler words instead of Latin. It swaps in words like "kerfuffle,"
				"bamboozle," and "flapjack" while keeping the same natural sentence
				rhythm as the Latin original.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				Every paragraph, ready to export
			</h3>
			<p>
				Once your text is generated, the live word and character counters
				update instantly so you know exactly what you're pasting. A single
				click copies the result to your clipboard — no manual select-all, no
				extra whitespace. Export as plain text for a copy deck, HTML with
				each paragraph already wrapped in a &lt;p&gt; tag for direct use in
				markup, or Markdown for a README, CMS field, or static site. Because
				everything runs client-side, there's no page reload and no waiting:
				change the theme, the amount, or the unit, and the output regenerates
				live.
			</p>

			<p>
				Whether you need one word for a button label, a full page of
				paragraphs for a content mockup, or an exact-length string for a
				character-limited field, this tool is built to handle all of it from a
				single form — free, instant, and with no sign-up required. Paragraph,
				sentence, word, and character controls are ready in one place whenever
				a layout needs filler text.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
